WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.936
Media capture.

00:00:01.886 --> 00:00:03.863
Who controls the narrative?

00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:12.000
The CBC receives approximately one point four billion dollars in annual parliamentary appropriation from the government it is supposed to critically cover.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:20.931
The six-hundred-million-dollar media bailout fund provides tax credits to qualifying news organisations, with eligibility determined by a government-appointed panel.

00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:30.920
Three companies — Bell, Rogers, and the Postmedia network — control the majority of Canadian media outlets, including television, radio, newspapers, and digital platforms.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:42.397
When the press is funded by the government, concentrated in three corporate hands, and regulated by the CRTC, editorial independence exists on paper but not in the structural incentives that drive coverage decisions.

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:54.193
This page documents the ownership structure, funding mechanisms, and regulatory framework that create Canada's media capture — all from CRTC filings, corporate annual reports, and parliamentary appropriations.

00:00:54.193 --> 00:00:56.272
Media concentration.

00:00:56.272 --> 00:01:00.068
Three corporate groups control the majority of Canadian media.

00:01:00.068 --> 00:01:06.863
Bell Media operates CTV, thirty radio stations, the Globe and Mail (partial), and multiple specialty channels.

00:01:06.863 --> 00:01:12.409
Rogers Media operates Citytv, Sportsnet, and multiple radio and digital platforms.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:23.499
Postmedia Network owns the National Post, the Ottawa Citizen, the Calgary Herald, the Vancouver Sun, the Edmonton Journal, the Montreal Gazette, and dozens of other daily and community newspapers.

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:30.647
This concentration means that editorial decisions at three corporate headquarters determine the news diet for the majority of Canadians.

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:40.068
When three companies control most of what Canadians read, watch, and hear, the diversity of perspectives is structurally limited regardless of individual journalistic integrity.

00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:42.443
Government funding mechanisms.

00:01:42.443 --> 00:01:45.863
Three funding streams create financial dependence.

00:01:45.863 --> 00:01:48.272
The information architecture.

00:01:48.272 --> 00:01:50.727
Three layers of media capture.

00:01:50.727 --> 00:01:55.818
Layer one: ownership concentration limits the diversity of editorial perspectives.

00:01:55.818 --> 00:02:00.454
Three corporate groups control most of what Canadians read, watch, and hear.

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:04.477
Layer two: government funding creates financial dependence.

00:02:04.477 --> 00:02:07.863
The CBC depends on government appropriation.

00:02:07.863 --> 00:02:11.443
Private media depends on government bailout programs.

00:02:11.443 --> 00:02:15.249
Both create incentives to avoid coverage that threatens funding.

00:02:15.249 --> 00:02:19.659
Layer three: regulatory control determines market access.

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:22.772
The CRTC decides who broadcasts.

00:02:22.772 --> 00:02:25.602
The government appoints the CRTC.

00:02:25.602 --> 00:02:27.590
The circle is complete.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.693
This is not censorship.

00:02:29.693 --> 00:02:32.352
It is structural incentive alignment.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:35.420
No editor is explicitly told what to cover.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:47.352
The funding structures, ownership concentration, and regulatory frameworks create an environment where certain coverage is systematically less likely — not because it is prohibited, but because the institutional incentives do not support it.

00:02:47.352 --> 00:02:49.636
The accountability connection.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:55.397
Media capture is the capstone of the institutional capture architecture documented across this site.

00:02:55.397 --> 00:03:08.079
If the military leadership is degraded, the courts cannot check power in real time, the Crown corporations operate outside oversight, the regulators serve industry, and the media is structurally dependent on the government — then who informs the public?

00:03:08.079 --> 00:03:15.590
When the information system that is supposed to hold power accountable is itself captured, the entire accountability architecture collapses.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:24.704
This is why TENET5 exists: because the institutions that are supposed to investigate and report on government failures are structurally unable or unwilling to do so.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:36.147
The documentation on this website comes from official government records, court decisions, and parliamentary reports — information that is publicly available but that the captured media ecosystem systematically under-reports.
